(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[30] = {"n":30,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 15% · B2 50% · B2+ 35%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"The search for life on Europa","p":["Of all the places in the solar system where scientists hope to find life beyond Earth, one of the most promising is not a planet at all, but a moon. Europa, one of the four large moons of Jupiter discovered by Galileo in 1610, is slightly smaller than our own Moon. Its surface is a frozen shell of ice, criss-crossed by long cracks and ridges, with a temperature of around minus 160 degrees Celsius. At first sight, it seems an unlikely place for living things. Yet beneath that ice, scientists believe, lies a vast ocean of liquid water.","The evidence for this hidden ocean has accumulated over several decades. Images from the Voyager and Galileo spacecraft revealed that Europa's surface is remarkably young and smooth, with very few impact craters, suggesting that it is constantly being renewed. Some areas, known as 'chaos terrain', look like broken blocks of ice that have shifted and refrozen, as if floating on something beneath. The strongest evidence came from measurements of Europa's magnetic field, which suggested the presence of a salty, electrically conducting layer under the surface, most likely an ocean. Scientists estimate that this ocean may contain more than twice as much water as all of Earth's oceans combined.","But how can water stay liquid so far from the Sun? The answer lies in Jupiter's powerful gravity. As Europa follows its slightly elliptical orbit, the changing pull of the giant planet stretches and squeezes the moon, generating heat through friction inside it, a process known as tidal heating. This heat may be enough to keep the ocean liquid beneath an ice shell perhaps fifteen to twenty-five kilometres thick.","Liquid water is only one of the ingredients that life as we know it requires. Life also needs certain chemical elements, such as carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen, and a source of energy. Scientists think that Europa's rocky seafloor could be in contact with the ocean, possibly releasing minerals and heat through hydrothermal vents similar to those found on Earth's ocean floor. On Earth, such vents support rich communities of organisms that obtain energy from chemicals rather than sunlight, showing that life can exist in complete darkness.","To investigate Europa further, NASA launched the Europa Clipper mission in 2024. Rather than landing, the spacecraft will orbit Jupiter and make dozens of close flybys of Europa, using cameras, radar and other instruments to measure the thickness of the ice, analyse the composition of the surface and search for possible plumes of water vapour escaping from cracks. The European Space Agency's JUICE mission will also study Europa, along with two other moons of Jupiter. If plumes are confirmed, future spacecraft might be able to sample the ocean's contents without drilling through kilometres of ice.","Even if these missions do not find life, they will greatly improve our understanding of how habitable environments can exist far from the warmth of a star. And if they do find evidence of life, even in the form of simple microorganisms, the implications would be profound. A second, independent origin of life in our own solar system would suggest that life may be common throughout the universe."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the discoveries of Galileo","why scientists think Europa might support life","how Jupiter was formed","the temperature of Earth's oceans"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains Europa's potential for life.","h":"إمكانية الحياة في أوروبا."},{"n":2,"s":"What does the small number of impact craters on Europa suggest?","o":["It is very old.","It has a thick atmosphere.","It is made entirely of rock.","Its surface is constantly being renewed."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"تجدد السطح."},{"n":3,"s":"Which evidence for the ocean does the writer describe as the strongest?","o":["measurements of Europa's magnetic field","images of chaos terrain","photographs from Galileo's telescope","samples brought back to Earth"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 states this.","h":"قياسات المجال المغناطيسي."},{"n":4,"s":"How does tidal heating keep Europa's ocean liquid?","o":["The Sun heats the surface directly.","Volcanoes on the surface melt the ice.","Jupiter's gravity stretches and squeezes the moon, producing heat.","The ice reflects heat downwards."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"التسخين المدّي."},{"n":5,"s":"The word 'elliptical' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["perfectly circular","oval-shaped","very fast","extremely cold"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"A slightly oval orbit.","h":"بيضاوي."},{"n":6,"s":"Why does the writer mention hydrothermal vents on Earth?","o":["to explain how ice forms","to describe tourist attractions","to compare Earth's and Jupiter's sizes","to show that life can exist without sunlight"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Organisms there use chemical energy.","h":"حياة بلا ضوء الشمس."},{"n":7,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'those' in 'similar to those found on Earth's ocean floor' refers to","o":["hydrothermal vents","minerals","organisms","chemical elements"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"Vents similar to Earth's vents.","h":"تعود على الفتحات الحرارية."},{"n":8,"s":"Why would plumes of water vapour be useful to scientists?","o":["They would melt the ice shell.","They might allow sampling of the ocean without drilling.","They would provide fuel for spacecraft.","They would make Europa warmer."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"أخذ عينات دون حفر."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a goal of the Europa Clipper mission?","o":["landing astronauts on the surface","measuring the thickness of the ice","analysing the surface composition","searching for plumes"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"It will not land.","h":"الهبوط غير مذكور."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's main point in the final paragraph?","o":["The missions are certain to find life.","The missions are a waste of money.","Life can only exist on Earth.","Finding life on Europa would suggest life may be common in the universe."],"k":3,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the last sentence.","h":"الحياة قد تكون شائعة."}]},{"t":"The Indus Valley civilization","p":["In the 1920s, archaeologists working in what is now Pakistan made a discovery that transformed the history of South Asia. At sites called Harappa and Mohenjo-daro, they uncovered the remains of large, carefully planned cities that were more than four thousand years old. These cities belonged to a civilization now known as the Indus Valley or Harappan civilization, which flourished between about 2600 and 1900 BCE along the Indus River and its tributaries. At its height, it covered a larger area than ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia combined.","The most striking feature of the Indus cities is their level of urban planning. Streets were laid out in a grid pattern, with main roads running north–south and east–west. Houses were built from baked bricks of standard sizes, and many had their own wells and bathrooms. Most remarkable of all was the drainage system: covered drains ran along the streets, carrying waste water away from homes, and could be accessed for cleaning. Few cities anywhere in the world had comparable sanitation until modern times. Mohenjo-daro also contained a large public structure known as the Great Bath, a waterproof pool that may have been used for ritual purposes.","The Indus people were skilled craftsmen and traders. They made fine pottery, jewellery from gold and semi-precious stones, and thousands of small square seals carved with animals and short inscriptions. These seals have been found as far away as Mesopotamia, indicating long-distance trade, much of which probably passed through the Gulf, including the trading centre of Dilmun. The use of standardized weights across the entire region suggests a high degree of economic organization.","Despite these achievements, much about the civilization remains mysterious. The Indus script, found on seals and pottery, has never been deciphered. The inscriptions are very short, usually only a few signs, and no bilingual text has been found to help scholars. As a result, we do not know what language the people spoke, what they called themselves or how they were governed. Interestingly, archaeologists have found no clear evidence of grand palaces, royal tombs or large temples, which has led some researchers to suggest that Indus society may have been organized differently from other early civilizations, perhaps with less concentrated power.","From around 1900 BCE, the major Indus cities began to decline and were gradually abandoned. Earlier theories proposed that the civilization was destroyed by invading peoples, but this idea is no longer widely accepted, as there is little evidence of large-scale violence. Many researchers now emphasize environmental change. Studies suggest that the monsoon rains weakened over several centuries, and some rivers that supported agriculture changed course or dried up. As farming became more difficult, people appear to have moved eastwards and southwards into smaller villages.","The Indus Valley civilization did not simply vanish without trace. Elements of its culture, such as certain crafts, farming practices and possibly religious symbols, may have continued in later South Asian societies. Its well-planned cities remain a remarkable example of what early urban societies could achieve, and its undeciphered script continues to challenge researchers around the world."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Rivers of Modern Pakistan","How to Decipher Ancient Scripts","The Planned Cities of an Ancient Civilization","The History of the Monsoon"],"k":2,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage describes the Indus civilization.","h":"مدن حضارة السند."},{"n":12,"s":"According to the passage, how large was the Indus civilization at its height?","o":["larger than Egypt and Mesopotamia combined","about the size of one modern city","smaller than ancient Egypt","the same size as Mesopotamia"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 states this.","h":"أكبر من مصر وبلاد الرافدين معًا."},{"n":13,"s":"Why does the writer describe the drainage system as remarkable?","o":["It was made of gold.","It carried water from the sea.","It was built by Egyptian engineers.","Few cities had comparable sanitation until modern times."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"نظام صرف متقدم."},{"n":14,"s":"The word 'standardized' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["very heavy","made according to a fixed pattern","decorated","rarely used"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Weights of uniform sizes.","h":"موحد."},{"n":15,"s":"What does the discovery of Indus seals in Mesopotamia indicate?","o":["Mesopotamia ruled the Indus Valley.","The seals were made in Mesopotamia.","The two regions spoke the same language.","The Indus people traded over long distances."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"تجارة بعيدة المدى."},{"n":16,"s":"Why has the Indus script not been deciphered?","o":["The signs have been destroyed.","Scholars have not studied it.","The inscriptions are short and there is no bilingual text.","It is identical to cuneiform."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"قصر النقوش وغياب نص ثنائي."},{"n":17,"s":"What can be inferred from the absence of grand palaces and royal tombs?","o":["Power may have been less concentrated than in other civilizations.","The Indus people had no government.","All the palaces were destroyed by invaders.","The cities were never inhabited."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Researchers suggest a different organization.","h":"سلطة أقل مركزية."},{"n":18,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'this idea' refers to","o":["the theory of climate change","the decline of the cities","the movement of people eastwards","the theory that invaders destroyed the civilization"],"k":3,"ty":"reference","e":"It refers to the invasion theory.","h":"تعود على نظرية الغزو."},{"n":19,"s":"What do many researchers now believe caused the decline of the Indus cities?","o":["a large-scale invasion","a single great flood","a disease brought by traders","environmental changes such as weaker monsoon rains"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"التغيرات البيئية."},{"n":20,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It describes the civilization's achievements, mysteries and decline.","It compares two modern countries.","It tells the life story of an archaeologist.","It gives instructions for visiting Pakistan."],"k":0,"ty":"organization","e":"The paragraphs follow this order.","h":"إنجازات وألغاز وأفول."}]},{"t":"Lifelong learning in ageing societies","p":["Across the world, populations are ageing. Thanks to better healthcare, nutrition and living conditions, people are living longer than ever before, while in many countries, families are having fewer children. In Japan, almost thirty per cent of the population is now over sixty-five, and many European countries are not far behind. Although the Gulf states currently have relatively young populations, they too will age significantly in the coming decades. These changes are prompting governments, employers and educators to rethink a long-standing assumption: that education is something we complete in our youth.","The traditional model of life was divided into three stages: education in childhood and early adulthood, followed by several decades of work, and then retirement. In a world of rapid technological change, this model is increasingly outdated. Skills learned at the age of twenty may become obsolete long before a person retires, especially as automation and artificial intelligence transform many occupations. If people are also expected to work until a later age, as many countries now require, they will need opportunities to update their skills or even change careers in their forties, fifties or sixties.","Lifelong learning refers to the continuous pursuit of knowledge and skills throughout life, whether for professional or personal reasons. It can take many forms: formal university courses, professional training, online programmes, community classes, or simply reading and learning independently. Some countries have introduced policies to encourage it. Singapore, for example, provides its citizens with credits that can be spent on approved courses throughout their working lives. Saudi Arabia's Human Capability Development Program, part of Vision 2030, also includes lifelong learning among its goals, aiming to equip citizens with the skills needed in a changing economy.","Research suggests that lifelong learning also brings benefits beyond employment. Older adults who remain mentally active, for example by learning a language or a musical instrument, tend to maintain better cognitive function. Learning in groups can reduce loneliness and isolation, which are serious health risks for older people. Many universities now offer programmes specifically designed for older learners, and in some countries, 'Universities of the Third Age' allow retired people to share their knowledge and learn from one another.","However, there are significant barriers. Many older adults lack confidence in their ability to learn, especially when courses involve new technology. Some employers are reluctant to invest in training older workers, assuming that they will retire soon or are less adaptable, assumptions that research does not always support. Cost and time can also be obstacles, particularly for people with family responsibilities. Moreover, those who would benefit most from further learning, such as workers with low levels of formal education, are often the least likely to participate.","Overcoming these barriers will require action from many different groups. Governments can provide funding and flexible pathways for adults to gain new qualifications, while employers can create a culture that values learning at every stage of a career. Educational institutions must design courses that suit adults' busy lives, with part-time, online and modular options. Above all, societies may need to change their attitudes, recognizing that the ability to learn does not end at any particular age. In an ageing world, learning throughout life is likely to become not a luxury but a necessity."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of universities in Japan","how to plan for retirement","why lifelong learning is becoming essential in ageing societies","why birth rates are rising"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage discusses lifelong learning.","h":"التعلم مدى الحياة."},{"n":22,"s":"Why are populations ageing, according to the passage?","o":["People live longer and families have fewer children.","More young people are emigrating.","Retirement ages are falling.","Healthcare is getting worse."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"طول العمر وقلة المواليد."},{"n":23,"s":"Why is the traditional three-stage model of life becoming outdated?","o":["People no longer need education.","Skills learned when young may become obsolete during a long career.","Retirement has been abolished.","Children start work earlier."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"تقادم المهارات."},{"n":24,"s":"The word 'obsolete' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["more valuable","easier to learn","no longer useful","widely used"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Outdated skills.","h":"متقادم."},{"n":25,"s":"Why does the writer mention Singapore?","o":["to show that Singapore has the oldest population","as an example of a policy that supports lifelong learning","to criticize its education system","to compare its weather with the Gulf"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"It provides learning credits.","h":"مثال على سياسة داعمة."},{"n":26,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of lifelong learning?","o":["better cognitive function","reduced loneliness","higher pensions","updated skills for employment"],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Pensions are not mentioned.","h":"المعاشات غير مذكورة."},{"n":27,"s":"What does the writer suggest about employers' assumptions regarding older workers?","o":["They are always correct.","They were proven by Universities of the Third Age.","They apply only to young workers.","They are not always supported by research."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"'assumptions that research does not always support'.","h":"افتراضات غير مدعومة."},{"n":28,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'those' in 'those who would benefit most' refers to","o":["employers","courses","barriers","people"],"k":3,"ty":"reference","e":"People who would benefit most.","h":"تعود على الأشخاص."},{"n":29,"s":"What is the purpose of paragraph 6?","o":["to describe the history of education","to suggest how different groups can overcome the barriers","to list the causes of ageing","to compare older and younger learners"],"k":1,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"It proposes actions for each group.","h":"اقتراح حلول."},{"n":30,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards lifelong learning?","o":["doubtful about its value","critical of older learners","strongly supportive","neutral and uninterested"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"It is 'not a luxury but a necessity'.","h":"داعم بقوة."}]},{"t":"Wildlife corridors","p":["As human populations grow, cities, farms, roads and railways spread across the landscape, dividing natural habitats into smaller and smaller pieces. This process, known as habitat fragmentation, is one of the greatest threats to wildlife. Even when patches of forest, grassland or desert remain, animals living in them may be cut off from food, water, mates and seasonal migration routes. Small, isolated populations are more vulnerable to disease, natural disasters and inbreeding, which reduces genetic diversity and makes populations less able to adapt to change.","One solution that conservationists have increasingly adopted is the creation of wildlife corridors: strips or networks of habitat that connect otherwise isolated areas, allowing animals to move safely between them. Corridors can take many forms, and their design depends on the species they are intended to help. Some are long stretches of natural vegetation along rivers or mountain ranges. Others are specially built structures, such as bridges covered with soil and plants that allow animals to cross busy highways, or tunnels running beneath roads.","Some of the best-known examples come from North America. In Banff National Park in Canada, engineers built a series of wildlife overpasses and underpasses across the Trans-Canada Highway. Cameras have recorded hundreds of thousands of crossings by animals such as bears, wolves, deer and elk, and collisions between vehicles and large animals have fallen dramatically. Similar structures have been built in Europe, Asia and Australia, where some are designed for animals as small as crabs and frogs.","Larger-scale corridors aim to connect entire ecosystems. In Central America, the Mesoamerican Biological Corridor seeks to link protected areas from southern Mexico to Panama. In Africa, conservation groups work with landowners and communities to keep migration routes open for elephants, whose traditional paths increasingly pass through farmland. Such projects require cooperation between governments, landowners and local people, since corridors often cross private land and national borders.","Corridors are not without critics. Some ecologists point out that corridors can also help invasive species, diseases and wildfires to spread. Others argue that resources might sometimes be better spent on protecting larger areas of habitat rather than connecting small ones. Measuring success can also be difficult: knowing that animals use a corridor does not necessarily prove that the corridor has improved the long-term survival of a population. For this reason, scientists emphasize careful planning based on the needs of particular species, along with long-term monitoring.","In Saudi Arabia, conservation efforts are increasingly focused on connecting landscapes. Large protected areas, such as the royal reserves and the conservation zones around AlUla, are helping to restore populations of native species like the Arabian oryx, gazelles and, in the future, the Arabian leopard. Planners are considering how fencing, roads and new developments can be designed to allow wildlife to move between these areas. As climate change forces many species to shift their ranges in search of suitable conditions, the ability to move across landscapes may become more important than ever, making wildlife corridors a key tool for conservation in the twenty-first century."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how highways are built in Canada","how wildlife corridors help animals in fragmented habitats","why elephants migrate","the history of national parks"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains wildlife corridors.","h":"ممرات الحياة البرية."},{"n":32,"s":"Why are small, isolated populations of animals at risk?","o":["They are more vulnerable to disease, disasters and inbreeding.","They grow too quickly.","They have too much food.","They are hunted more often."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"الضعف أمام المخاطر."},{"n":33,"s":"Which of the following is an example of a built wildlife corridor?","o":["a fence around a farm","a car park near a forest","a zoo in a city","a bridge covered with soil and plants over a highway"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 gives this example.","h":"جسر مغطى بالنباتات."},{"n":34,"s":"What has happened since the overpasses were built in Banff National Park?","o":["Collisions between vehicles and large animals have fallen dramatically.","The number of animals has decreased.","The highway has been closed.","Animals refuse to use the structures."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"انخفاض الاصطدامات."},{"n":35,"s":"Why do large-scale corridor projects require cooperation?","o":["Animals cannot cross borders.","Governments refuse to fund them.","Corridors often cross private land and national borders.","They are built only in cities."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"عبور أراضٍ وحدود."},{"n":36,"s":"The word 'dramatically' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["greatly and noticeably","slightly","slowly","surprisingly little"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"A large fall.","h":"بشكل كبير."},{"n":37,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a criticism of corridors?","o":["They can help invasive species spread.","Money might be better spent on larger areas.","They are too beautiful to be useful.","Their success is difficult to measure."],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Beauty is not mentioned.","h":"الجمال غير مذكور."},{"n":38,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'whose' in 'whose traditional paths increasingly pass through farmland' refers to","o":["conservation groups","elephants","landowners","migration routes"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"The elephants' paths pass through farmland.","h":"تعود على الفيلة."},{"n":39,"s":"What can be inferred about the Arabian leopard?","o":["It is already common in AlUla.","There are plans to restore its population in the future.","It has been extinct for centuries.","It does not need protected areas."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"'in the future, the Arabian leopard'.","h":"خطط لإعادته."},{"n":40,"s":"Why might wildlife corridors become more important in the future?","o":["Cities will become smaller.","Roads will disappear.","Climate change may force species to move to new areas.","Animals will stop migrating."],"k":2,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"The final sentence explains this.","h":"التغير المناخي."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Little ______ that the small company would one day become a global brand.","o":["did anyone imagine","anyone imagined","imagined anyone","anyone did imagine"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did anyone imagine — Little at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Little."},{"n":42,"s":"If I had listened to my teacher's advice, I ______ in a much better position now.","o":["would have been","will be","would be","am"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would be — Mixed conditional: past condition, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":43,"s":"______ by the results of the survey, the managers changed their strategy.","o":["Alarmed","Alarming","To alarm","Having alarming"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Alarmed — Past participle clause for reason.","h":"اسم المفعول."},{"n":44,"s":"The regulations require that every visitor ______ an identity card.","o":["carries","carrying","will carry","carry"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"carry — Subjunctive after require that.","h":"صيغة الطلب."},{"n":45,"s":"The ancient city is estimated ______ around fifty thousand people at its peak.","o":["to have","having had","that it had","to have had"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to have had — Passive reporting about the past.","h":"is estimated to have had."},{"n":46,"s":"She wishes she ______ able to attend her brother's graduation next week.","o":["is","were","will be","has been"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were — wish + were for a present/future unreal situation.","h":"wish + were."},{"n":47,"s":"What impressed the judges ______ the originality of her design.","o":["were","it was","was","being"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was — Cleft sentence with What.","h":"What … was."},{"n":48,"s":"Only after the storm had passed ______ the extent of the damage.","o":["the residents saw","saw the residents","did the residents see","the residents did see"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did the residents see — Only after: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Only after."},{"n":49,"s":"The library received a donation of five hundred books, many of ______ are rare.","o":["which","them","whom","that"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — many of which.","h":"many of which."},{"n":50,"s":"______ it not for the support of my family, I couldn't have completed my degree.","o":["Had","Were","Should","If"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Were — Were it not for = if it weren't for.","h":"Were it not for."},{"n":51,"s":"By the time she retired, she ______ at the school for over thirty years.","o":["has taught","taught","was teaching","had taught"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had taught — Past perfect with by the time.","h":"الماضي التام."},{"n":52,"s":"The athlete was disappointed at not ______ for the national team.","o":["selecting","being selected","be selected","to be selected"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"being selected — Preposition + passive gerund.","h":"being + p.p."},{"n":53,"s":"The new policy will benefit students regardless ______ their background.","o":["to","of","for","with"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"of — regardless of.","h":"regardless of."},{"n":54,"s":"______ the team plays well, they may still lose against such strong opponents.","o":["Unless","Provided","Even if","As long as"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Even if — Even if = whether or not.","h":"Even if."},{"n":55,"s":"He behaves as if he ______ the owner of the company.","o":["is being","will be","has been","were"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"were — as if + were for unreal comparison.","h":"as if + were."},{"n":56,"s":"The new stadium ______ when the world cup begins in 2034.","o":["will complete","will have been completed","has completed","is completing"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have been completed — Future perfect passive.","h":"المستقبل التام المجهول."},{"n":57,"s":"______ the high cost of living, many young people still choose to live in the capital.","o":["Although","Whereas","In spite of","Even though"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"In spite of — In spite of + noun phrase.","h":"In spite of."},{"n":58,"s":"The guide advised the visitors ______ plenty of water on the hike.","o":["to carry","carrying","carry","that carrying"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to carry — advise + object + to-infinitive.","h":"advise sb to."},{"n":59,"s":"The higher you climb, ______ the air becomes.","o":["the thinner","thinner","the thinnest","more thin"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the thinner — Double comparative.","h":"the … the."},{"n":60,"s":"We would rather ______ the train than drive through the mountains at night.","o":["taking","to take","took","take"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"take — would rather + base verb.","h":"would rather."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"Neither of the (A) twins (B) like (C) spicy (D) food.","o":["twins","like","spicy","food"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"like — Neither of + singular verb: likes.","h":"Neither of مفرد."},{"n":62,"s":"The research (A) was (B) carried out by a team (C) who's members (D) came from five countries.","o":["was","carried out","who's","came"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"who's — Possession: whose.","h":"whose للملكية."},{"n":63,"s":"(A) Since two years, she (B) has (C) been studying (D) medicine.","o":["Since","has","been","medicine"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Since — Duration: For two years.","h":"For مع المدة."},{"n":64,"s":"The workers (A) were (B) made (C) to wait for hours (D) without to eat.","o":["were","made","to wait","without to eat"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"without to eat — without + -ing: without eating.","h":"without + -ing."},{"n":65,"s":"I (A) have never seen (B) such (C) an beautiful (D) sunset.","o":["have","such","an","sunset"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"an — Before a consonant sound: a.","h":"a قبل الحرف الساكن."},{"n":66,"s":"Hardly (A) had the meeting (B) begin when the (C) lights (D) went out.","o":["had","begin","lights","went"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"begin — Past perfect: had begun.","h":"begun."},{"n":67,"s":"The professor gave us (A) some useful (B) advice (C) on how to write (D) an essays.","o":["some","advice","on","an essays"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"an essays — an + singular noun: an essay.","h":"an essay."},{"n":68,"s":"(A) Me and my brother (B) are going (C) to the (D) match tonight.","o":["Me","are","to","match"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Me — Subject pronoun: My brother and I.","h":"ضمير الفاعل."},{"n":69,"s":"The weather was (A) so (B) hot that we (C) can't (D) play outside.","o":["so","hot","can't","play"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"can't — Past context: couldn't.","h":"couldn't."},{"n":70,"s":"There (A) were (B) less cars on the road (C) than (D) usual.","o":["were","less","than","usual"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"less — cars is countable: fewer.","h":"fewer مع المعدود."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M30-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What does the second man suggest about the speech?","o":["It was long and boring.","It was inspiring.","It was too short.","It was difficult to hear."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"He mentions checking his watch and someone sleeping.","h":"طويلة ومملة."}],"t":"A hint that a speech was boring","sc":["Situation: Two friends are leaving a graduation ceremony. (62 words)","Man: What did you think of the guest speaker?","Man: Well, he certainly had a lot to say. I think I checked my watch about ten times, and the man next to me fell asleep halfway through.","Man: It was nearly an hour long, wasn't it?","Man: Fifty-five minutes. The organizers had asked him to speak for fifteen. Still, the graduates seemed happy at the end."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M30-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What is the woman doing?","o":["politely declining additional work for now","accepting the task immediately","asking for a promotion","complaining about her colleagues"],"k":0,"ty":"function","e":"She explains her workload and offers alternatives.","h":"رفض مهذب."}],"t":"A careful refusal of extra work","sc":["Situation: A manager speaks to an employee. (66 words)","Woman: Lina, could you also take charge of organizing the staff training day next month?","Woman: I'd really like to help, but I'm already leading the budget review and the new client project, which both finish next month.","Woman: I see. That is a lot.","Woman: If it could wait until the following month, I'd be happy to do it. Or perhaps I could support whoever takes it on now."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M30-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What is the second man concerned about?","o":["the location of Jeddah","the date of the conference","the number of staff","the cost of the conference"],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"He mentions the reduced budget.","h":"التكلفة."}],"t":"A manager's hidden concern about costs","sc":["Situation: Two managers are discussing a proposal. (64 words)","Man: I'd like to hold this year's staff conference at a five-star hotel in Jeddah.","Man: It's a lovely idea. Have you looked at what we spent last year, though? And the budget has been reduced by fifteen per cent this year.","Man: Do you think it's too expensive?","Man: Let's just say we should compare it with a few other options, perhaps including our own conference hall."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M30-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What is the second woman's opinion of the novel?","o":["She loved every part of it.","She did not finish it.","She enjoyed most of it but disliked the ending.","She disliked the characters."],"k":2,"ty":"opinion","e":"Gripping first half, disappointing ending.","h":"رأي مختلط."}],"t":"A friend's mixed review of a book","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking about a novel. (61 words)","Woman: Did you finish the novel I lent you?","Woman: I did. The first half was gripping. I couldn't put it down. But the ending was disappointing. It felt rushed, as if the writer had run out of ideas.","Woman: Really? I loved the ending.","Woman: Well, the characters were wonderful, so I'd still recommend it. I just wish the last chapter had been longer."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M30-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What is the second man questioning?","o":["whether customers are satisfied","whether the report was written on time","whether the company has ten thousand customers","whether the survey sample was large and representative enough"],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"He asks about the sample size and selection.","h":"حجم العينة."}],"t":"A polite challenge to a statistic","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are discussing a report. (54 words)","Man: According to this report, eighty per cent of customers are satisfied with our service.","Man: That's encouraging. May I ask how many customers were surveyed?","Man: Let me check. Fifty, I think.","Man: Out of more than ten thousand customers? I wonder whether that sample is large enough to draw firm conclusions. And how were they chosen?"]},{"rec":6,"a":"M30-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"What does the second woman mean by 'on the home stretch'?","o":["She has just started.","She is working from home.","She is close to finishing.","She has given up."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"She has finished all the chapters.","h":"قاربت على الانتهاء."}],"t":"An idiom about finishing a project","sc":["Situation: Two students are talking about their research. (51 words)","Woman: How's your dissertation going?","Woman: I'm on the home stretch. I've finished all the chapters, and now I'm just checking the references and formatting.","Woman: That's great. When do you have to submit it?","Woman: Next Thursday. I'll be so relieved when it's finally done. It's taken me almost two years of hard work."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M30-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What does the second man mean by 'My lips are sealed'?","o":["He will keep the secret.","He cannot talk because he is ill.","He does not want to come.","He will tell Majed."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"He promises not to tell.","h":"سيحفظ السر."}],"t":"A request to keep a secret about a surprise party","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking quietly. (53 words)","Man: We're organizing a surprise party for Majed's retirement next Thursday. Can you come?","Man: Of course. What time?","Man: Four o'clock in the meeting room. But please don't say a word to him. If he asks, just tell him there's a department meeting.","Man: My lips are sealed. I'll bring a card for everyone to sign."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M30-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What does the second woman think about the forecast?","o":["It is completely accurate.","It predicts heavy rain.","It may not be reliable for the mountains.","It is for the wrong city. Longer conversations"],"k":2,"ty":"opinion","e":"Mountain weather changes quickly.","h":"قد لا تكون دقيقة."}],"t":"Doubt about a weather forecast","sc":["Situation: Two friends are planning a trip. (62 words)","Woman: The forecast says it'll be sunny all weekend in Abha. Perfect for our hike.","Woman: I wouldn't rely on that too much. The weather in the mountains can change very quickly, especially in the afternoon.","Woman: So what should we do?","Woman: Let's start early in the morning and pack rain jackets, just in case. And we should check the forecast again before we leave."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M30-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"What is the main inconsistency in the thesis?","o":["The analysis chapter is too short.","The introduction mentions three research questions, but the conclusion discusses only two.","The thesis has no abstract.","The methods were not described."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated by the supervisor.","h":"عدم اتساق الأسئلة."},{"n":80,"s":"Why does the supervisor advise her to explain the change in her methods?","o":["Examiners appreciate honesty about how research develops.","It will make the thesis shorter.","The university requires a new title.","Her abstract is too long."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated by the supervisor.","h":"الأمانة في عرض التطور."}],"t":"Discussing final revisions to a thesis","sc":["Situation: A student meets her supervisor. (175 words)","Woman: Dr. Nasser, thank you for reading my final draft. Is it ready to submit?","Man: Almost. It's a strong thesis, and your analysis chapter is excellent. But there are a few things to fix before submission.","Woman: Of course. What are they?","Man: First, your introduction promises to examine three research questions, but the conclusion only discusses two. You need to address the third or revise the introduction.","Woman: I see. I think I dropped the third question when I changed my methods.","Man: Then explain that change in the introduction and the methodology chapter. Examiners appreciate honesty about how research develops.","Woman: What else?","Man: Your abstract is too long. It should be no more than three hundred words. And please check that every source in the text appears in the reference list. I found several missing.","Woman: I'll go through them carefully. How long do you think the revisions will take?","Man: About a week, if you work steadily. Send me the revised version by next Sunday, and I'll check it quickly.","Woman: Thank you. I really appreciate your help throughout this project."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M30-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"How do they plan to solve the problem of limited space?","o":["by moving the ceremony outdoors","by limiting guests and using a second hall with a video stream","by holding two separate ceremonies","by reducing the number of graduates"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Explained in the conversation.","h":"حد للضيوف وقاعة ثانية."},{"n":82,"s":"Why is the speaker's request for a ten-minute speech welcomed?","o":["The auditorium closes early.","Long speeches were the main complaint last year.","The speaker is not well known.","Graduates prefer no speeches."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the conversation.","h":"تجنب الشكوى السابقة."}],"t":"Planning a graduation ceremony","sc":["Situation: Two members of a student committee are planning a ceremony. (171 words)","Man: The graduation ceremony is in six weeks. Let's go through what we still need to arrange.","Man: The venue is booked: the main auditorium, which holds twelve hundred people. But we have nearly four hundred graduates, and each wants to bring at least three guests.","Man: That's a problem. We could limit each graduate to two guests in the auditorium and set up a second hall with a live video stream for additional family members.","Man: Good idea. We could also stream it online for relatives who can't travel.","Man: Who's giving the main speech?","Man: The committee suggested inviting a former student who now runs a successful technology company. She's agreed, but she's asked to keep the speech to ten minutes.","Man: Perfect. Long speeches were the main complaint last year.","Man: We also need volunteers to guide guests to their seats and help elderly visitors.","Man: I'll ask the student volunteer club. And let's do a full rehearsal with the graduates two days before.","Man: Agreed. I'll send the schedule to everyone by the end of the week."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M30-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Why does the first woman think Sara is a better choice?","o":["Sara has the highest grades.","Reem refuses to work with others.","Reliability matters more in a long project.","Sara lives closer to the university."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the conversation.","h":"الموثوقية أهم."},{"n":84,"s":"What does the first woman say about disagreements?","o":["They should be avoided completely.","They show that partners are unsuitable.","They should be reported to the professor.","They are normal; how they are handled is what matters. Talks and lectures"],"k":3,"ty":"opinion","e":"Stated in the conversation.","h":"طبيعية والمهم إدارتها."}],"t":"Choosing a lab partner for a long project","sc":["Situation: Two students are discussing a year-long project. (175 words)","Woman: We have to choose a partner for the year-long research project by Thursday. Have you decided?","Woman: I'm thinking about it. Reem is brilliant, but she's often late and sometimes misses meetings. Sara is very reliable, though her grades aren't quite as high.","Woman: For a project that lasts a whole year, I'd say reliability matters more than being brilliant. You'll be depending on each other every week.","Woman: That's a good point. And Sara is very organized. She always plans everything in advance.","Woman: Also, think about how well you communicate. Do you get on well with her?","Woman: Yes, we worked together on a short assignment last term, and it went smoothly. We disagreed sometimes, but we always found a solution.","Woman: That sounds ideal. Disagreements are normal; what matters is how you handle them.","Woman: You're right. I'll ask Sara tomorrow before someone else does.","Woman: Good luck. And make sure you agree on how you'll divide the work from the beginning.","Woman: Definitely. We could write a short plan with deadlines for each stage and share it with our supervisor."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M30-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"What is one of the most promising benefits of AI in education, according to the lecturer?","o":["replacing all teachers","making classes larger","removing the need for exams","personalized learning adapted to each student"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"التعلم المخصص."},{"n":86,"s":"Why are many educators redesigning assessments?","o":["to make exams easier","to focus on the learning process so students develop their own skills","to save money on paper","to allow more use of AI in exams"],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Explained in the lecture.","h":"التركيز على عملية التعلم."},{"n":87,"s":"What is the lecturer's main message?","o":["AI should support rather than replace good teaching.","AI will soon replace schools.","AI should be banned from classrooms.","AI is always accurate."],"k":0,"ty":"gist","e":"Stated at the end.","h":"الدعم لا الاستبدال."}],"t":"Extended lecture: the future of artificial intelligence in education","sc":["Situation: An education lecturer speaks to students. (303 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today, we'll consider how artificial intelligence, or AI, may change education in the coming years. AI tools are already widely used by students and teachers, and their influence is likely to grow. Let's begin with the potential benefits. One of the most promising is personalized learning. In a typical class of thirty, a teacher cannot always adjust lessons to every student's level and pace. AI tutoring systems can analyse a student's answers, identify specific misunderstandings and provide explanations and practice tailored to that individual. Research on earlier intelligent tutoring systems found that well-designed programmes could produce significant learning gains, particularly in subjects such as mathematics. AI can also reduce teachers' workload. Tools that help with planning lessons, creating quizzes or giving initial feedback on writing may free teachers to spend more time on what humans do best: motivating students, building relationships and guiding discussion. However, there are serious challenges. The first concerns accuracy: AI systems can produce confident but incorrect information, so students must learn to verify what they read. The second concerns learning itself. If students use AI to complete assignments without thinking, they may lose opportunities to develop essential skills such as writing, problem-solving and critical thinking. As a result, many educators are redesigning assessments to focus more on the process of learning, such as oral explanations, drafts and work done in class. A third challenge is fairness and privacy. AI systems collect large amounts of data about students, raising questions about how that data is stored and used. Unequal access to technology could also widen gaps between students. Saudi Arabia has established national bodies and guidelines to promote the responsible use of AI, including in education. The key message is that AI should support, not replace, good teaching. Its value will depend on how thoughtfully it is used."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M30-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"What are ephemeral plants?","o":["plants that live for hundreds of years","plants that grow only in water","plants that complete their life cycle in a few weeks","plants that never produce flowers"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Defined in the lecture.","h":"نباتات قصيرة العمر."},{"n":89,"s":"Why do many desert seeds not germinate after a light shower?","o":["They are eaten by insects first.","They need sunlight to germinate.","They are buried too deeply.","A chemical coating prevents germination until there is enough rain."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"Explained in the lecture.","h":"غلاف كيميائي."},{"n":90,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention off-road driving?","o":["to recommend it as a way to see the flowers","to show how human activity can harm desert blooms","to explain how seeds are spread","to describe a sport in the Najd region"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"It damages plants before seeds form.","h":"إظهار الضرر البشري."}],"t":"Lecture: how deserts can bloom after rain","sc":["Situation: A botany lecturer speaks to students. (298 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. Deserts are often imagined as lifeless places of sand and rock. Yet after significant rainfall, some deserts are transformed almost overnight, as plants that seemed absent suddenly cover the ground with green leaves and colourful flowers. This phenomenon is sometimes called a desert bloom. How is this possible? The answer lies in the remarkable adaptations of desert plants, particularly a group known as ephemerals or annuals. These plants complete their entire life cycle, from seed to flower to new seed, in just a few weeks. Their seeds can remain dormant in the soil for years, sometimes decades, waiting for the right conditions. Many seeds have chemical coatings that prevent them from germinating after a light shower, which would not provide enough water for the plant to survive. Only a substantial rainfall washes away these chemicals and triggers germination. This strategy is sometimes described as a way of 'avoiding' drought rather than resisting it. Once the seeds germinate, the plants grow extremely quickly, flower and produce new seeds before the soil dries out again. The seeds then return to the soil, forming what scientists call a seed bank, ready for the next rain. Other desert plants use different strategies. Some perennials survive dry periods by storing water in thick stems or roots, while others drop their leaves to reduce water loss and grow new ones when rain returns. In Saudi Arabia, after good winter rains, areas such as the northern deserts and parts of the Najd region can become covered with grasses and wild flowers, providing grazing for animals and attracting visitors. These blooms are not only beautiful but ecologically important, supporting insects, birds and other animals. However, they are also fragile: off-road driving and overgrazing can damage plants before they have produced seeds, reducing future blooms."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Some tourists prefer beaches.","Visas can be applied for online.","Museums are often closed on Mondays.","Tourism is therefore becoming an increasingly important part of the Kingdom's economy."],"x":["Saudi Arabia has invested heavily in tourism in recent years. New visa rules have made it easier for international visitors to enter the country, and major projects are creating resorts, museums and cultural sites. The number of visitors has grown rapidly as a result. ______"],"k":3,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It draws a conclusion from the details.","h":"خلاصة منطقية."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Good sleep is essential for students' academic success. (2) It helps the brain to store new information. (3) It improves concentration during lessons. (4) Many students enjoy playing video games. (5) Students who sleep well also tend to have better moods."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Video games do not develop the point about sleep.","h":"ألعاب الفيديو خارج الفكرة."},{"n":93,"s":"(A) Many of the furniture in the old house (B) was (C) made of (D) wood.","o":["Many","was","made","wood"],"k":0,"ty":"error","e":"furniture is uncountable: Much.","h":"Much مع غير المعدود."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["For all these reasons, cooking is a skill worth learning.","Some kitchens are very small.","Restaurants are popular at weekends.","Recipes can be found online."],"x":["Learning to cook offers many advantages. It allows people to eat healthier meals, save money and share food with family and friends. It can even be a relaxing way to spend time after a busy day. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It sums up the advantages.","h":"خلاصة الفوائد."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Mangrove forests are important coastal ecosystems. (2) They protect shorelines from erosion and storms. (3) They provide breeding grounds for many fish species. (4) Some beaches are popular with tourists. (5) Mangroves also absorb large amounts of carbon dioxide."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Tourist beaches do not develop the point about mangroves.","h":"الشواطئ السياحية خارج الفكرة."},{"n":96,"s":"The engineers (A) managed (B) to finish the bridge (C) ahead of schedule, despite the (D) badly weather.","o":["managed","to","ahead","badly"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"An adjective is needed before the noun: bad.","h":"الصفة bad."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Beaches can become very dirty.","Volunteers contribute to their communities in many different ways.","Food banks open in the morning.","Many children enjoy reading."],"x":["______ Volunteers clean beaches, plant trees and help at food banks. Others visit elderly people or teach children to read."],"k":1,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details list ways volunteers help.","h":"أشكال التطوع."},{"n":98,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 1 – 3 – 4","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) After that, the clay pots are decorated with traditional patterns.","(2) Pottery-making in the village begins with collecting clay from the riverbank.","(3) The clay is then cleaned and shaped on a wheel.","(4) Finally, the pots are fired in a kiln to make them strong."],"k":1,"ty":"best order","e":"begins → then → After that → Finally.","h":"ترتيب الخطوات."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["They enjoy the freedom to organize their own day and avoid long commutes.","Offices often have meeting rooms.","Some homes have gardens.","Computers can be expensive."],"x":["Many people dream of working from home. ______ However, others find it difficult to stay motivated without colleagues around them."],"k":0,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"An advantage before 'However'.","h":"ميزة قبل However."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["However","Otherwise","In contrast","For example Teacher Section — Mock 30"],"x":["The new law aims to reduce plastic waste. ______, shops must now charge customers for plastic bags."],"k":3,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence gives an example.","h":"For example للمثال."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
